.\" keel(1) — KEEL high-performance database proxy
.\" ============================================================================
.TH KEEL 1 "2026-04-17" "KEEL 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.\" ============================================================================
.SH NAME
keel \- high-performance database connection proxy for PostgreSQL and MySQL
.\" ============================================================================
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keel
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-H
.IR host ]
.RB [ \-P
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.br
.B keel \-\-help
.br
.B keel \-\-version
.\" ============================================================================
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B keel
is a zero-copy, thread-per-core database connection proxy supporting both
PostgreSQL (wire protocol v3) and MySQL (client/server protocol) from a single
binary.
It provides transaction-level connection pooling, automatic read/write
splitting, TLS termination with kernel TLS (kTLS) acceleration, prepared
statement virtualization, and session state preservation across backend
reassignments.
.PP
In normal operation,
.B keel
reads its configuration from an INI file (see
.BR keel.ini (5))
and binds one or more worker groups to the specified network ports.
Each worker group runs on a dedicated thread with its own
.BR io_uring (7)
ring, connection pool, and SQL routing engine.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file" "\fR,\fP \-\-config " file
Read configuration from
.IR file .
If not specified, keel looks for
.I /etc/keel/keel.ini
by default.
.TP
.BI \-l " addr" "\fR,\fP \-\-listen " addr
Bind to
.IR addr
(default:
.BR 0.0.0.0 ).
.TP
.BI \-p " port" "\fR,\fP \-\-port " port
Listen on
.IR port
(default:
.BR 6432 ).
.TP
.BI \-H " host" "\fR,\fP \-\-host " host
Backend database host (default:
.BR 127.0.0.1 ).
.TP
.BI \-P " port" "\fR,\fP \-\-backend\-port " port
Backend database port (default:
.BR 5432 ).
.TP
.BI \-w " n" "\fR,\fP \-\-workers " n
Number of worker threads.
Default is the number of online CPU cores.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase log verbosity.
Can be specified multiple times (e.g.\&
.BR \-vvv ).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Fork into the background and detach from the controlling terminal.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version, build, and backend information, then exit.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload configuration: pool sizes, timeouts, server weights, probe timing,
rebalancing parameters, TLS certificates, and log level.
Equivalent to the
.B RELOAD
admin command.
.TP
.B SIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM
Initiate graceful shutdown.
In-flight transactions are allowed to complete up to
.I shutdown_timeout_ms
(default: 30 000 ms), after which remaining connections are force-closed.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Dump runtime statistics to the log at INFO level.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/keel/keel.ini
Default configuration file.
See
.BR keel.ini (5).
.TP
.I /etc/keel/userlist.txt
Client authentication credential file (username/password pairs).
.TP
.I /etc/keel/certs/
Default directory for TLS certificate material.
Use
.B scripts/generate\-test\-certs.sh
to generate development certificates, or provide production certificates
signed by a trusted CA.
.TP
.I /var/log/keel/
Default log directory when file logging is enabled.
.TP
.I /var/run/keel/keel.pid
PID file when running as a daemon.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH PORTS
.TP
.B 7432
Default PostgreSQL proxy port (configurable via worker group).
.TP
.B 7306
Default MySQL proxy port (configurable via worker group).
.TP
.B 6433
Admin console port (PgBouncer-compatible wire protocol).
.TP
.B 9101
Prometheus metrics HTTP endpoint (/metrics, /healthz, /readyz, /livez).
.\" ============================================================================
.SH ADMIN CONSOLE
Connect to the admin port with any PostgreSQL client:
.PP
.RS
.nf
psql -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6433 -U admin -d keel
.fi
.RE
.PP
Available commands include
.BR "SHOW POOLS" ,
.BR "SHOW CLIENTS" ,
.BR "SHOW SERVERS" ,
.BR "SHOW STATS" ,
.BR "SHOW CONFIG" ,
.BR "PAUSE" ,
.BR "RESUME" ,
.BR "RELOAD" ,
.BR "DRAIN" ,
.BR "KILL CLIENT" ,
.BR "DISABLE SERVER" ,
.BR "ENABLE SERVER" ,
.BR "ADD SERVER" ,
.BR "REMOVE SERVER" ,
and
.BR "SET" .
Append
.B FORMAT JSON
to any command for JSON output.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH EXAMPLES
Start with a configuration file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keel -c /etc/keel/keel.ini
.fi
.RE
.PP
Quick test with command-line overrides:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keel -p 6432 -H localhost -P 5432 -w 4
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run as a daemon:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keel -d -c /etc/keel/keel.ini
.fi
.RE
.\" ============================================================================
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown.
.TP
.B 1
Startup failure (configuration error, bind failure, etc.).
.\" ============================================================================
